WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.317 --> 00:00:06.969
At that moment the instruments in the gallery indicated that only a thin sheet of ice separated us from open water.

2
00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:09.705
I was desperate to breathe some fresh air.

3
00:00:09.706 --> 00:00:16.197
Once again I heard the sound of the water tanks filling, but this time it was only the tanks in the stern.

4
00:00:16.198 --> 00:00:24.031
As the bow of the submarine began to rise, I realized that the Nautilus was going to attempt to break through the ice above us.

5
00:00:24.032 --> 00:00:30.015
The submarine attacked the ice with its battering ram, and then it backed off and rushed forward again.

6
00:00:30.016 --> 00:00:34.191
This time the icy crust gave way and we shot to the surface.

7
00:00:34.192 --> 00:00:40.279
Someone tore open the hatches and pure air came rushing into all parts of the Nautilus

8
00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.183
. I have no idea how I got to the platform; perhaps Ned carried me

9
00:00:45.184 --> 00:00:49.350
there. But once I was out in the fresh sea air, I breathed

10
00:00:49.351 --> 00:00:53.501
deeply. "How delightful this air is!" said

11
00:00:53.502 --> 00:00:57.834
Conseil. "And we may all breathe freely because there is enough for

12
00:00:57.835 --> 00:01:04.088
everyone." Ned did not say anything but inhaled deeply, opening his jaws wide enough to frighten a

13
00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:10.255
shark. When our strength returned, I tried to express my gratitude to Ned and Conseil for saving my life.

14
00:01:10.256 --> 00:01:17.311
"My friends," I said, "we will be bonded by this experience forever, and I have a great obligation to you both."

15
00:01:17.312 --> 00:01:21.414
"Which I will take advantage of," Ned said slyly.

16
00:01:21.415 --> 00:01:24.247
"What do you mean?" asked Conseil.

17
00:01:24.248 --> 00:01:30.406
"Only that I will insist on taking the professor and you with me when I leave this infernal submarine."

18
00:01:30.407 --> 00:01:35.803
"Well," said Conseil, "after all this, are we heading in the right direction?"

19
00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:40.565
"Yes, because we're definitely headed north," I replied.

20
00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:46.871
"But it remains to be seen," said Ned, "whether Captain Nemo will take us into the Atlantic or the Pacific Ocean.

21
00:01:46.872 --> 00:01:52.636
In other words, are we going toward seas that are heavily traveled or seas that are lonely?"

22
00:01:52.637 --> 00:02:01.453
I could not answer that question and feared that Captain Nemo would prefer to take us to the Pacific Ocean, so that he could complete his underwater tour of the world.

23
00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:06.541
But by March 31 it was clear that we were heading into the Atlantic.

24
00:02:06.542 --> 00:02:16.676
By April 12 we had crossed the equator and were within twenty miles of Guiana, a French territory that would have given refuge to Ned, Conseil, and me.

25
00:02:16.677 --> 00:02:21.866
But a stiff breeze was blowing and the waves would have capsized the small boat.

26
00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:28.517
Ned must have realized this because he made no mention of escaping. For my part, I said nothing either.

27
00:02:28.518 --> 00:02:32.866
I didn't want to encourage Ned in an attempt to escape that would fail.

28
00:02:32.867 --> 00:02:39.184
To Ned's great disappointment, the Nautilus stayed out of the Caribbean Sea and the Gulf of Mexico.

29
00:02:39.185 --> 00:02:45.597
He had hoped either to land on one of the islands or to hail one of the many boats passing from island to island.

30
00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:51.581
That assumed, of course, that he was able to launch the small boat without Captain Nemo's knowledge.

31
00:02:51.582 --> 00:02:57.001
On April 16 we sighted Martinique and Guadeloupe from a distance.

32
00:02:57.002 --> 00:03:03.398
We had been prisoners on the Nautilus for five months and had traveled seventeen thousand leagues.

33
00:03:03.399 --> 00:03:08.398
"This voyage will never come to an end!" Ned moaned one day in despair.

34
00:03:08.399 --> 00:03:13.501
"We're going to have to be the ones who end it because that captain never will."

35
00:03:13.502 --> 00:03:19.977
Meanwhile what concerned me was that Captain Nemo had become more serious and less sociable.

36
00:03:19.978 --> 00:03:22.119
I rarely saw him anymore.

37
00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:29.874
He used to spend time explaining underwater marvels to me; these days he left me to my studies and never came into the gallery.

38
00:03:29.875 --> 00:03:32.195
My attitude had changed too. I

39
00:03:32.196 --> 00:03:40.874
realized that I now had the research to write a solid book about underwater life, and I wanted to get back to land to see that book published. On

40
00:03:40.875 --> 00:03:45.199
April 20 we were cruising underwater near the Bahamas. Tall

41
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.533
cliffs rose beneath the sea, their surfaces covered with weeds. I

42
00:03:50.534 --> 00:03:54.279
was looking out the gallery windows with Conseil and Ned. "Giant

43
00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:58.461
squid could live in some of those caves," I suggested. "Oh,

44
00:03:58.462 --> 00:04:02.834
I'll never believe that such animals exist," said Ned. "Well,"

45
00:04:02.835 --> 00:04:11.096
Conseil said very seriously, "I remember seeing a large vessel being pulled under the waves by a squid's tentacles." "Oh,

46
00:04:11.097 --> 00:04:16.517
really," Ned said skeptically, "and exactly where was that?" "In

47
00:04:16.518 --> 00:04:21.921
a church in Brittany," said Conseil. "There's a painting showing that scene." "He's

48
00:04:21.922 --> 00:04:26.255
right," I said as Ned and I turned away from the windows. "The

49
00:04:26.256 --> 00:04:33.993
painting, however, is based merely on a legend about a squid that was supposed to be a mile long, more like an island than an animal. Another

50
00:04:33.994 --> 00:04:43.660
legend tells of a bishop building an altar on a large rock; after the bishop said mass, the rock—which was a giant squid—walked into the sea." "How

51
00:04:43.661 --> 00:04:47.239
much of those stories is true?" asked Conseil. "When

52
00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:51.977
it comes to sea monsters, imaginations run wild," I replied. "The

53
00:04:51.978 --> 00:04:55.713
legends are untrue, but huge squids do exist. Some

54
00:04:55.714 --> 00:05:02.136
naturalists have calculated that a six-foot squid would have tentacles twenty-seven feet long." Ned

55
00:05:02.137 --> 00:05:10.882
was especially interested in how someone would catch one of these creatures, so I told him about an unsuccessful attempt that occurred a few years before. "How

56
00:05:10.883 --> 00:05:13.953
big was this squid?" asked Ned. "Wasn't

57
00:05:13.954 --> 00:05:17.707
it about 18 feet?" said Conseil. "And

58
00:05:17.708 --> 00:05:22.628
when it waved its tentacles about, didn't they look like a nest of serpents? And

59
00:05:22.629 --> 00:05:25.453
didn't it have a beak like a parrot's?" "That's

60
00:05:25.454 --> 00:05:28.120
all correct," I said. "Well,

61
00:05:28.121 --> 00:05:35.620
then, I think it—or its brother—is right outside the window! Look!" Ned

62
00:05:35.621 --> 00:05:44.858
and I rushed to the glass, where we saw an immense squid that had to be at least twenty-five feet long and four thousand pounds. What

63
00:05:44.859 --> 00:05:53.820
a freak of nature! It had hundreds of suckers along its tentacles and its beaklike mouth was lined with rows of sharp teeth. Nearby

64
00:05:53.821 --> 00:05:56.517
were more squid! I

65
00:05:56.518 --> 00:05:59.596
counted seven by the light of the Nautilus .

66
00:05:59.597 --> 00:06:05.747
Suddenly the submarine stopped. A shock made it tremble from bow to stern.

67
00:06:05.748 --> 00:06:09.596
"We can't have hit anything because we're still floating," said Ned. Captain

68
00:06:09.597 --> 00:06:14.342
Nemo and his lieutenant came into the gallery and looked through the windows. "The

69
00:06:14.343 --> 00:06:18.588
squid have disabled our propeller," Captain Nemo explained. "We're

70
00:06:18.589 --> 00:06:22.659
going to rise to the surface and see if we can get rid of them." As

71
00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:28.238
the submarine rose, we followed Nemo up the stairs that led to the platform. There,

72
00:06:28.239 --> 00:06:33.897
about ten men were waiting, armed with hatchets and ready to attack. Conseil

73
00:06:33.898 --> 00:06:37.826
and I picked up hatchets while Ned seized a harpoon. One

74
00:06:37.827 --> 00:06:42.072
of the crewmen unscrewed the bolts of the hatch and opened it. Immediately

75
00:06:42.073 --> 00:06:46.556
a huge tentacle slid down and grabbed the man. "Help!

76
00:06:46.557 --> 00:06:50.135
Help!" he cried in French. I

77
00:06:50.136 --> 00:06:55.215
had a fellow countryman on board, and I hadn't even known it until this awful moment! Captain

78
00:06:55.216 --> 00:07:01.127
Nemo and his crew fought the squid, but it blinded them with a stream of its black liquid. By

79
00:07:01.128 --> 00:07:07.286
the time the men could see again, the squid had disappeared overboard with the unfortunate crewman. Ten

80
00:07:07.287 --> 00:07:12.277
or twelve squid now climbed up the sides and platform of the Nautilus .

81
00:07:12.278 --> 00:07:19.389
Ned fought them off with his harpoon until one pinned him to the platform with its horrible, heavy tentacles.

